\v 6 If¦92385 \add ≈you¦92415 happen to¦92391 find\add* a bird's¦92389 nest in a tree¦92395 or on the ground, and the mother bird is¦92405 sitting in the nest on its¦92387 eggs¦92412 or with¦92392 the baby birds¦92389, don't¦92413 take¦92415 \add ≈both the mother bird and the babies\add*.
\v 7 You¦92422 may¦92433 take¦92429 the \add ≈eggs or the baby birds\add*, but¦92426 you must¦92422 \add ≈allow the mother¦92425 bird to¦92431 fly away\add*. Do that so that things will¦92429 go¦92433 well¦92433 for you and¦92426 so that you'll¦92422 live \add in the land\add* for a long time¦92436.
\p
\v 8 When you build¦92440 a new¦92442 house¦92450, be sure to include a railing around your¦92445 \add flat\add* roof¦92445, so you won't¦92446 \add ≈be guilty of¦92450 having caused someone's death\add* if¦92451 a person falls¦92454 off it¦92455 \add and dies\add*.
\p
\v 9 Don't¦92458 \add ≈plant a crop\add* in your¦92461 vineyard, because then the harvest¦92466 of¦92461 the crop and¦92470 the fruit from the vineyard will¦92460 \add both\add* \add ?≈be¦92465 defiled\add*.\f + \fr 22:9 \ft Or possibly, become sacred.\f*\x + \xo 22:9-11: \xt Lev 19:19.\x*
\p
\v 10 Don't¦92474 plough with¦92477 a cow¦92477 and¦92479 a donkey¦92479 \add harnessed\add* together¦92480.
\p
\v 11 Don't¦92483 wear¦92484 \add clothes\add* with a mix of wool¦92486 and¦92487 linen¦92487.
\p
\v 12 \add Twist threads together to\add* make¦92492 tassels¦92491 and \add sew them\add* onto the¦92497 four¦92497 corners¦92498 of¦92498 your¦92499 cloak¦92499.\x + \xo 22:12: \xt Num 15:37-41.\x*
\s1 Rules about marriage and rape
\rem /s1 Laws concerning Sexual Relations; Marriage Violations; Regulations for Sexual Purity; Laws concerning Sexual Purity
\p
\v 13 If¦92506 a man¦92509 takes¦92508 a wife¦92510 and¦92511 \add ≈sleeps with\add* her, then hates¦92513 her
\v 14 and accuses her of¦92517 shameful things, \add ≈destroying her reputation¦92521\add* and \add ≈claiming that¦92527 she hadn't been\add* a virgin¦92535,
\v 15 then¦92537 the young¦92539 woman's¦92539 parents¦92541 must¦92537 show the evidence of¦92545 her¦92541 virginity¦92545\f + \fr 22:15 \ft Presumably, the blood-stained sheet.\f* to¦92548 the elders¦92550 at the city¦92551 gate¦92552.
\v 16 The¦92567 young¦92556 woman's¦92556 father¦92555 must tell the elders¦92563 how he gave¦92567 his daughter¦92566 to¦92561 the man¦92568 as a wife, but¦92554 now he's¦92571 rejected her¦92571
\v 17 and made¦92576 shameful accusations about¦92577 her not¦92580 being¦92586 a virgin¦92584. Then¦92582 the parents must¦92588 spread¦92588 out¦92588 that cloth¦92589 and show it to¦92590 the city¦92592 elders¦92591.
\v 18 Then¦92594 the¦92595 city¦92596 elders¦92595 must¦92594 punish¦92602 that¦92598 man¦92601
\v 19 and fine¦92605 him¦92606 a hundred¦92607 silver¦92608 \add coins\add* to be paid to the¦92610 young¦92611 woman's¦92611 father¦92610 for¦92612 defaming an Israeli¦92618 virgin¦92617. Then¦92605 she'll¦92621 remain as his¦92632 wife and he'll¦92625 be prohibited from \add ever\add* \add ≈divorcing¦92626\add* her¦92626.
\p
\v 20 \add ≈However,\add* if¦92635 \add @the accusation¦92643\add* was¦92638 true and¦92635 the young¦92645 woman¦92645 \add ≈hadn't been a virgin¦92644\add*,
\v 21 then¦92648 they¦92648 must take \add *her\add* to¦92653 the doorway of¦92655 her father's house. Then¦92648 the men¦92660 from that city¦92661 must \add ≈execute her by¦92669 throwing rocks¦92662 at¦92662 her\add*, because¦92664 she's¦92663 done¦92666 a disgraceful¦92667 thing in Yisrael¦92668—committing fornication \add ≈while she still lived in\add* her father's house. That's how you'll¦92672 remove¦92672 evil¦92673 from \add ≈your¦92674 country\add*.
\p
\v 22 If¦92677 a¦92680 man is discovered sleeping with¦92683 another man's¦92693 wife, then both¦92692 of¦92692 them¦92692 must¦92679 \add ≈be¦92679 executed\add*. That's¦92694 how you'll¦92699 remove¦92699 evil¦92700 from¦92701 Yisrael¦92701.
\p
\v 23 If¦92704 a young¦92706 woman¦92706 is¦92705 engaged¦92709 to¦92710 a man, and¦92711 \add another\add* man sleeps with¦92713 her¦92711,
\v 24 then¦92717 you¦92756 must¦92717 execute both¦92720 of¦92720 them at the city¦92725 gate¦92723 by throwing rocks¦92729 at them—the young¦92733 woman¦92733 because she¦92741 didn't¦92739 scream, and¦92717 the man for¦92741 defiling another man's¦92745 \add ≈fiancée\add*.
\p
\v 25 If¦92761 the man found¦92764 the young¦92768 woman¦92768 \add out\add* in¦92773 the countryside¦92763 and \add ≈overpowers\add* her and \add ≈rapes\add* her, then the man \add ≈must¦92764 be¦92783 executed\add*
\v 26 but¦92785 don't¦92789 do¦92791 anything¦92792 to that¦92809 woman¦92785 because¦92798 she's not¦92789 worthy of death¦92797. (This¦92809 is¦92807 similar to the case of a man murdering¦92805 another man \add out in the countryside\add*.)
\v 27 When that man found¦92813 her \add out\add* in¦92812 the¦92815 countryside¦92812, then she¦92814 would have cried¦92814 out \add for help\add*, but¦92818 no \add ≈one was¦92818 there¦92818 to¦92820 save\add* her.
\p
\v 28 If¦92823 a man¦92826 \add ≈overpowers\add* a young¦92827 woman¦92827 who's¦92830 not¦92831 engaged¦92833, and \add ≈rapes\add* her¦92834, and it's¦92836 discovered¦92837,\x + \xo 22:28-29: \xt Exo 22:16-17.\x*
\v 29 that¦92841 man¦92840 must¦92850 pay her father¦92843 fifty¦92846 silver¦92847 coins, and¦92839 she'll¦92850 become his¦92864 wife because¦92852 he's¦92854 violated¦92854 her. He'll¦92857 then¦92839 be¦92857 prohibited from \add ever\add* \add ≈divorcing¦92858\add* her.
\p
\v 30 A man must never \add ≈sleep with\add* any of his father's wives \add ?≈because that would expose his father's nakedness\add*.\x + \xo 22:30: \xt Lev 18:8; 20:11; Deu 27:20.\x*
\c 23
\s1 Excluded people
\rem /s1 Exclusion From the Assembly; Regulations concerning Worship; Those Excluded from the Assembly; Exclusion from the Lord's People
\p
\v 1 \add ≈No man¦92871 with crushed testicles\add* or \add ≈a severed penis\add* is allowed to enter into Yahweh's assembly.
\p
\v 2 No \add ?≈illegitimate child\add*\f + \fr 23:2 \ft Or possibly, child from mixed parentage.\f* is¦92886 allowed to enter into Yahweh's¦92892 assembly¦92891, even down to the¦92891 tenth generation.
\p
\v 3 No Ammonite or Moabite is allowed to¦92908 enter¦92898 into Yahweh's¦92901 assembly¦92909, even down past the¦92900 tenth¦92904 generation¦92903\x + \xo 23:3-5: \xt Neh 13:1-2.\x*
\v 4 since they wouldn't \add even sell\add* you bread and¦92918 water when the¦92919 people were travelling from Egypt \add to Canaan\add*. \add ≈What's more,\add* they hired Beor's son Bil'am \add !(Balaam)\add* (from Petor in¦92919 Aram-Naharayim) to curse you
\v 5 \add ≈However,\add* your god Yahweh \add ≈took no notice of¦92937\add* Bil'am and¦92944 turned the¦92954 curse¦92960 into a blessing because \add *he¦92949\add* loves you,\x + \xo 23:5: \xt Num 23:7–24:9.\x*
\v 6 \add ≈so\add* you¦92975 must \add ≈never\add* strive for peace or prosperity for \add @the Ammonites¦92965 or the Moabites¦92965\add*.
\p
\v 7 \add In contrast,\add* don't despise the Edomites because they're your \add ≈cousins\add* \add (descendants of your ancestor Yitshak)\add*. And¦92990 don't despise the Egyptians, because you¦92988 \add ≈lived there\add* as foreigners.
\v 8 The third generation \add !(grandchildren)\add* of any of \add @Edomites¦93004 or¦93004 Egyptians¦93004\add* that live among you¦93000, may enter Yahweh's assembly.
\s1 Purity when at war
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Regulations; Keeping the Military Camp Clean; Uncleanness in the Camp; Sanitary, Ritual, and Humanitarian Precepts
\p
\v 9 When¦93027 \add @your warriors¦93020\add* are¦93020 out besieging your enemies, they¦93020 you must¦93020 avoid \add ≈ceremonial impurity\add*.
\v 10 If¦93031 something happens at night to make a man ‘unclean’, then he must¦93033 stay outside¦93033 the camp¦93034 \add the next day\add* \add ≈and¦93038 not join the others\add*.
\v 11 That¦93049 evening, he must¦93046 wash himself in¦93047 water, then once the sun has set, he may reenter the camp¦93067.
\p
\v 12 You must¦93074 have a place outside your camp¦93082 where you can go¦93076 to¦93071 \add relieve yourself\add*.
\v 13 You¦93091 must¦93087 have a spade among your equipment so that when you go¦93091 to¦93088 relieve yourself¦93088, you can dig a hole and¦93086 then cover it¦93087 up afterwards,
\v 14 because your god Yahweh walks in¦93103 your camp to give you victory \add ≈to defeat\add* your enemies, so your camp must¦93097 be¦93102 sacred so he won't see anything ‘unclean’ and¦93102 turn¦93107 away from you.
\s1 Various regulations
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Laws; Various Laws
\p
\v 15 You¦93115 mustn't \add ≈return\add* any slaves to their master if¦93114 they escaped and¦93122 got to you¦93135.
\v 16 \add ≈Allow them to¦93143 stay\add* \add ≈in your town\add* and don't¦93139 \add ≈mistreat\add* them.
\p
\v 17 \add ≈Don't¦93165 allow any Israeli man¦93161 or woman to¦93164 become a religious\add*\f + \fr 23:17 \ft Or, cultic.\f* prostitute.\x + \xo 23:17: \xt Lev 19:29.\x*
\v 18 \add ≈Don't¦93170 allow anyone woman¦93174 or man to bring any¦93174 part of¦93181 their wages\add* as a \add secular\add* prostitute¦93173 into your god Yahweh's temple, because both \add @female¦93173 and¦93176 male¦93179 prostitution¦93179\add* is abhorrent to \add *him\add*.
\p
\v 19 You¦93187 mustn't charge interest to¦93195 your¦93201 fellow Israeli, whether it's money or food or anything else \add ≈that's borrowed\add*.\x + \xo 23:19-20: \xt Exo 22:25; Lev 25:36-37; Deu 15:7-11.\x*
\v 20 You¦93209 can charge¦93209 foreigners interest¦93209, but not¦93207 \add fellow\add* Israelis so that your god Yahweh will¦93220 bless \add @your society\add* in everything you do in the land that you're about¦93210 to¦93210 occupy.
\s1 Making promises to God
\p
\v 21 When you make a vow to¦93225 your¦93225 god¦93231 Yahweh¦93230, don't¦93226 put off fulfilling it, because \add *he¦93229\add* will¦93224 \add ≈hold you accountable\add* and¦93225 \add not doing it\add* would \add count as\add* \add ≈disobeying him\add*.
\v 22 However \add of course\add*, \add ≈if¦93248 you don't¦93254 make any vows, then you won't¦93254 be¦93264 guilty\add*,
\v 23 \add ≈but¦93269\add* if \add ≈you've spoken your promise out loud\add* to your god Yahweh, then you're \add ≈obligated to keep it¦93274\add*.
\p
\v 24 If¦93285 you're walking through \add ≈someone else's\add* vineyard, you can eat as many grapes as \add ≈you like\add*, but¦93282 you mustn't pick any to¦93285 \add take away\add* in¦93290 a container.
\v 25 \add ≈Similarly,\add* if¦93294 you're going¦93295 through \add ≈someone else's\add* field of grain, you can \add crunch\add* \add ≈a few kernels, but¦93298\add* you mustn't \add ≈cut off any of the¦93296 stalks\add*.
\c 24
\s1 Divorce and remarriage
\rem /s1 Divorce and Remarriage; Laws concerning Marriage and Divorce
\p
\v 1 When a man¦93328 marries¦93330 a woman¦93329, then \add ≈further on¦93338 he decides he doesn't want her because¦93325 of something he found¦93341 out about¦93338\add* her, then he must¦93327 write¦93345 a divorce¦93348 notice and give¦93349 it¦93349 to¦93346 her, and send¦93351 her out of his¦93338 house¦93352.\x + \xo 24:1: \xt Mat 5:31; 19:7; Mrk 10:4.\x*
\v 2 \add ≈After she¦93354 leaves¦93354 that marriage\add*, \add ≈another¦93360 man¦93358 is allowed to¦93358 marry her\add*.
\v 3 If¦93374 that \add ≈second\add* husband \add also\add* \add ≈decides to divorce her by sending¦93371 her away¦93371 with¦93370 a divorce notice\add*, or¦93373 if¦93374 he¦93375 were to die¦93375,
\v 4 then the first¦93388 husband¦93387 isn't¦93406 allow to take¦93393 her¦93387 back as his wife. \add That's\add* because¦93400 she's¦93399 been¦93399 defiled and it¦93403 would be¦93394 abhorrent to Yahweh, and you mustn't defile the land¦93410 that¦93411 your¦93413 god¦93413 Yahweh is¦93414 giving¦93414 you.
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Laws; Various Laws
\p
\v 5 When a man¦93422 \add ≈is newly married\add*, he¦93421 mustn't \add ≈be called¦93419 for\add* the army¦93427 \add ≈or be given any¦93432 other responsibilities\add*. He¦93436 must¦93421 be free¦93435 for one¦93439 year¦93438 \add ≈to establish his house¦93437\add* and¦93440 \add ≈to satisfy his new¦93424\add* wife¦93423.
\p
\v 6 \add If someone borrows money,\add* \add @the lender\add* mustn't take¦93451 their millstone \add for grinding grain\add* as¦93458 a pledge¦93458, because¦93454 \add ≈they need it¦93457 to stay alive\add*.
\s1 Abduction
\p
\v 7 If¦93461 someone¦93464 is found¦93463 to have abducted a fellow Israeli¦93469 to make them into a slave¦93470 or to sell¦93473 them, then that¦93476 abductor must¦93463 \add ≈be¦93463 executed\add* in¦93472 order to purge¦93477 such evils from¦93479 \add ≈your¦93479 country¦93467\add*.\x + \xo 24:7: \xt Exo 21:16.\x*
\s1 Skin diseases
\p
\v 8 Stay on¦93482 guard¦93485 against a \add ≈deadly skin¦93484 disease¦93484\add*. Always follow what the¦93482 priests¦93493 and¦93487 Levites¦93494 tell you all¦93488 to¦93482 do¦93487, as¦93495 I've instructed¦93491 them.\x + \xo 24:8: \xt Lev 13:1–14:54.\x*
\v 9 Remember¦93501 what your¦93507 god¦93507 Yahweh¦93506 did¦93505 to¦93508 Miryam on¦93509 the way¦93509 out¦93510 of¦93507 Egypt¦93511 \add (Heb. Mitsrayim)\add*.\x + \xo 24:9: \xt Num 12:9-10.\x*
\p
\v 10 If¦93514 you've lent¦93516 anything¦93520 to a neighbour¦93517, don't¦93521 go¦93523 into¦93524 their house¦93526 to collect¦93527 their pledge¦93527.
\v 11 \add Just\add* \add ≈wait\add* outside¦93530, then the \add ≈person¦93532\add* you've lent¦93535 something to will¦93535 bring¦93537 the \add ≈item\add* out¦93537 to you.
\v 12 \add ≈However,\add* if¦93544 \add ≈that person¦93546\add* is¦93546 poor¦93547 \add and gives clothing or a blanket as security\add*, don't¦93549 \add ≈keep it overnight\add*.
\v 13 Take it¦93555 back to¦93555 them when the¦93558 sun¦93560 goes¦93559 down¦93561 so they'll \add be warm\add* when they sleep¦93561. Then they'll bless¦93563 you and¦93564 your¦93569 god¦93569 Yahweh¦93568 \add ≈will¦93554 be pleased with¦93562\add* you.
\p
\v 14 Don't¦93572 oppress¦93574 any poor¦93576 or needy¦93577 hired¦93575 workers, whether they're fellow Israelis or foreigners who¦93581 live in¦93582 your¦93580 towns¦93583.\x + \xo 24:14-15: \xt Lev 19:13.\x*
\v 15 At the¦93592 end of¦93587 each day¦93585, pay¦93586 them before sunset, because¦93593 they need it¦93596 to live on¦93605. Then they won't¦93588 cry out¦93604 to Yahweh¦93608 against¦93605 you and¦93609 have him¦93591 \add ≈hold you accountable\add*.
\p
\v 16 Parents¦93617 mustn't be executed for what their children have done, and¦93621 children mustn't be executed for what their parents¦93617 have done. Any person must¦93616 only be executed for what they¦93616 themselves have done.\x + \xo 24:16: \xt 2Ki 14:6; 2Ch 25:4; Eze 18:20.\x*
\p
\v 17 Don't¦93636 pervert justice¦93638 if it involves foreigners or orphans, and¦93641 don't¦93636 take¦93642 a widow's¦93644 cloak as¦93642 security \add for a loan\add*.\x + \xo 24:17-18: \xt Exo 23:9; Lev 19:33-34; Deu 27:19.\x*
\v 18 Remember¦93646 that¦93664 you were¦93649 a slave¦93648 in Egypt¦93650 and¦93646 your¦93659 god¦93653 Yahweh¦93652 \add ≈rescued\add* you from¦93654 there¦93654. \add ≈That's¦93647 why\add* I'm¦93658 commanding¦93659 you to do¦93660 those things¦93663—\add ≈to be considerate about¦93653 helping other people who are in need\add*.
\p
\v 19 When you \add ≈harvest¦93668 your crops\add*, if¦93667 you forget¦93671 a tied bundle in¦93670 your field¦93673, don't¦93674 go back to get it—it'll¦93680 be for a \add resident\add* foreigner or an orphan¦93678 or widow¦93679 \add to pick up\add*, so¦93681 that your god¦93684 Yahweh¦93683 can bless¦93682 everything \add ≈that you do\add*.\x + \xo 24:19-21: \xt Lev 19:9-10; 23:22.\x*
\v 20 When you¦93690 beat¦93690 your¦93691 olive¦93691 tree \add to make the fruit fall\add*, don't¦93692 go¦93693 back over¦93693 it¦93698 to pick the ones still on the tree. Those will¦93698 be for the foreigner, or the orphan¦93696 or widow¦93697.
\v 21 \add Similarly,\add* when you¦93702 \add ≈pick your¦93703 grapes¦93702\add* in the vineyard¦93703, don't¦93704 \add ≈go back over the vines a second time\add*. Those will¦93702 be for¦93701 the foreigner, or the orphan¦93708 or widow¦93709.
\v 22 \add ^Don't forget\add* that you were¦93716 a slave¦93715 in¦93717 Egypt¦93718—that's¦93713 why I'm¦93722 instructing you to do¦93724 those things¦93727.
\c 25
\s1 Whipping for criminals
\p
\v 1 If¦93731 \add ≈two parties have a dispute¦93734, they¦93737 can request a judgement¦93740 and the¦93740 judges¦93741 will¦93741 decide¦93741 who's right¦93742 and wrong\add*.
\v 2 If¦93752 \add ≈the guilty¦93756 person is¦93754 deserving of punishment\add*, the judge will¦93759 order him¦93759 to¦93761 lie¦93757 on¦93763 the ground \add ≈where he'll¦93757 be whipped in¦93763 front¦93760 of the judge as the crime deserves\add*.
\v 3 He¦93766 may not¦93767 \add ≈receive more than forty lashes¦93766\add* because that would be¦93778 \add ≈too degrading¦93778\add*.
\p
\v 4 Don't¦93783 \add ≈cover your cow's mouth when¦93787 it's¦93787 pulling the grain threshing¦93787 sledge\add*.\x + \xo 25:4: \xt 1Cor 9:9; 1Tim 5:18.\x*
\s1 Continuing the family name
\rem /s1 Levirate Marriage; Duty to a Dead Brother
\p
\v 5 If¦93790 some brothers¦93793 live \add ≈on¦93813 the same property\add* and one¦93796 of them¦93797 dies¦93795 without leaving a son¦93798, then the widow mustn't marry outside¦93808 \add ≈of the family\add*. Her¦93811 brother-in-law must¦93792 take¦93814 her¦93811 as¦93817 a wife¦93805 for¦93816 himself and \add ≈perform¦93817 the duty of\add* a brother-in-law.\x + \xo 25:5-6: \xt Mat 22:24; Mrk 12:19; Luk 20:28.\x*
\v 6 If she¦93822 gives birth to a son \add in due course\add*, he'll¦93823 be¦93831 considered as the son of¦93826 the brother¦93827 who died¦93828, so that his¦93832 \add family\add* name¦93832 won't¦93829 disappear from¦93833 Yisrael¦93833.
\v 7 \add ≈But¦93835 if¦93835 the dead man's¦93839 brother doesn't want to marry the widow\add*, she¦93844 must¦93838 go¦93844 to the elders¦93849 at¦93856 the city gate¦93846 and¦93835 \add ≈explain that her brother-in-law¦93852 is¦93858 refusing¦93851 to do his¦93854 duty to preserve\add* the dead brother's¦93859 \add family\add* name¦93855 in Yisrael¦93856.\x + \xo 25:7-10: \xt Rut 4:7-8.\x*
\v 8 Then¦93861 those elders¦93864 must¦93861 summon¦93861 \add @the¦93864 brother\add* and¦93861 talk to him¦93868. \add ≈However¦93872,\add* if he¦93869 \add ≈really\add* doesn't want to marry¦93873 her¦93873,
\v 9 then¦93875 his \add late\add* brother's wife¦93876 must go up to him¦93877 in front¦93885 of¦93878 those elders¦93879, and take his sandal¦93881 off¦93880 his foot¦93883 and spit¦93884 in his face¦93885. Then¦93875 she¦93886 must say¦93887, ‘That's what happens to a man¦93890 who¦93891 won't¦93892 \add ≈strengthen his brother's family\add*.’
\v 10 \add After that,\add* the brother will¦93902 be¦93902 known in¦93904 Yisrael¦93904 as the man who had his¦93903 sandal¦93907 taken off¦93906.
\s1 Being fair and honest
\rem /s1 Various Commands; Other Laws
\p
\v 11 If¦93910 two men¦93913 are fighting each other, and one¦93919 man's wife¦93918 comes¦93917 to¦93920 help her¦93927 husband¦93923, and reaches out¦93926 and grabs the¦93918 other man's private parts,
\v 12 then¦93931 you¦93931 must¦93931 cut¦93931 her hand¦93934 off¦93931. Don't¦93935 pity¦93936 her.
\s1 Don't all cheat
\p
\v 13 Don't¦93940 keep two stone \add weights\add* in¦93944 your¦93944 bag—a \add ≈lighter one\add* and a \add ≈heavier one\add* \add for when you're buying and selling\add*.\x + \xo 25:13-16: \xt Lev 19:35-36.\x*
\v 14 Don't¦93951 keep \add ≈two measuring¦93956 containers\add* in¦93955 your¦93955 house¦93955—a smaller¦93959 \add one\add* and¦93957 a larger¦93958 \add one\add*.
\v 15 You¦93966 must¦93964 use exact stone \add weights\add* and¦93969 \add ≈measuring¦93967 containers\add* so¦93973 that your god¦93981 Yahweh¦93980 will¦93964 prolong your time¦93975 in the¦93977 \add ≈country\add* that he's giving¦93982 you—
\v 16 \add *he\add* \add ≈can't¦93991 stand¦93991 people¦93991 who¦93991 cheat¦93991 others¦93991\add*.
\s1 Eliminating the Amalekites
\rem /s1 The Command to Kill the Amalekites
\p
